(Regression test for bug #44: a format string used as a function argument
 is the last sink LANGUAGE.md's "Format Strings Everywhere" names, and it
 goes through the same expression materializer a text initializer uses, so
 a `{list}` argument arrived inside the function as a heap address in
 decimal.

 The rendered text must also be a real, self-contained string: the caller's
 list can be appended to afterwards without the text it produced changing,
 because "each evaluation allocates a new string".

 Acceptance: the function prints the same rendering Print does, and the
 text taken before the append still reads [1, 2, 3] after it.)

To 'announce the text' with a text called line.
  Print line.

a list called flat is [1, 2, 3].
a map called person is {"name": "Ada", "born": 1815}.

Print "argument: {flat}".
'announce the text' of "argument: {flat}".

Print "argument: {person}".
'announce the text' of "argument: {person}".

a text called snapshot is "{flat}".
append 4 to flat.
Print "the list now: {flat}".
Print "the snapshot still: {snapshot}".
